description   "A volcanic water cooled from 55 C to 20 C: which minerals can precipitate, and how much? Each operation is an INDEPENDENT equilibrium at its own temperature on the SAME analysis (nothing removed between them) -- not a sequential cooling that withdraws the solids as they form. Amorphous silica (SiO2a) is undersaturated at 55 and 45 C and precipitates from 35 C down (0 -> 50 mg/kg); calcite, whose solubility FALLS as T rises, precipitates more hot than cold (106 -> 69 mg/kg); gypsum stays dissolved. Quartz and chalcedony are far more supersaturated than amorphous silica at every temperature, and are kinetically inhibited at these temperatures -- allowing quartz instead shows how much the answer depends on WHICH silica phase the student lets form. The amounts are an EQUILIBRIUM CEILING, not a deposit prediction. The analysis is ILLUSTRATIVE, not a measured spring.";
verbosity     2;
